From 755ba31d5ae5b1a3bb1e9013efaa156bb5e7b8ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Avery Felts <averyfelts@Averys-Mac-Mini.local>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 01:24:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 41/44] Windows: Use MAP_JIT code buffers on macOS

MetalSharp's Wine can back the emulator's code buffers with MAP_JIT
instead of emulating W+X memory with a fault and mprotect() calls on
every code write. Ask for it at startup (ARM64EC and WoW64), and when
granted, open a per-thread write window with
pthread_jit_write_protect_np() (through the unix library) around every
write to JIT memory: copying a compiled block in, loading cached code,
linking and unlinking blocks, emitting the dispatcher, applying code
cache relocations, and backpatching unaligned accesses (whose lock
word lives in the block tail). The cache flush no longer needs Wine.
Windows must not nest; thread-local storage isn't available early
enough to count them.

Cold-start probe, 8000 functions: 269 to 93 ms with Multiblock off,
96 to 54 ms with it on; eight threads running the same code: 852 to
270 ms and 198 to 82 ms; 32-bit: 287 to 93 ms. Results match.
METALSHARP_NO_MAPJIT=1 keeps the emulated W+X memory.
